FE-3113 negative control — reset clears the cache; refresh only refetches.

Target spec:  modules/scenarios/runtime/components/__tests__/forced-surface.client-notifications.spec.ts
              (drives the shared forced-surface.harness.ts over the
              client-notifications scenario)
Target cases: "client-notifications — every preset the picker offers changes the page" >
              "armed loading"          — the loading preset must draw skeletons
              "armed error-collection" — the failed read must replace the rows
              ("armed empty" stays GREEN under this mutant: the refetched empty
              read carries no rows, so there is nothing stale to keep.)
Mutation:     swaps the published `reset` action from `resetQuery()` to
              `refetch()` on all three reads — reset now behaves as refresh:
              it re-reads but KEEPS the cached rows instead of removing them.
Why RED:      the harness boots LIVE, arms a preset over the cached rows, then
              calls `port.actions.reset` to return the surface to the preset's
              state. With refetch the stale rows survive, so:
              - "armed loading" never re-enters isLoading, so no Skeleton draws
                and the old records stay;
              - "armed error-collection" draws the error over stale rows
                (witness != [], rows not < live).
              Only cache removal returns the surface to the state the preset
              names, which is exactly what this mutant strips.
Authored by:  developer seat (mutant only), anchored to 62b851458. The mutated
              file is headless core; the source was edited only to capture this
              hunk and immediately reverted.
Verified by:  prover seat, blind — apply, run the three target cases RED, revert.

